WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.214
A Minimum Spanning Tree connects all V vertices of a weighted undirected graph using V minus one edges of minimum total weight.

00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:22.200
Removing any tree edge splits the graph, while adding any non-tree edge creates a unique cycle.By the cut property, the lightest edge crossing any partition cut belongs to an MST.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.676
Kruskal's algorithm sorts all edges by non-decreasing weight and scans them greedily.

00:00:28.676 --> 00:00:38.849
Use a Disjoint Set Union to test whether an edge's endpoints already belong to the same component; if not, unite them and add the edge cost.

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.700
Stop as soon as V minus one edges have been accepted.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:52.161
Prim's algorithm grows a single tree from a starting vertex by repeatedly annexing the cheapest edge reaching an unvisited vertex.

00:00:52.161 --> 00:01:00.124
It mirrors Dijkstra's heap loop except the priority is the single edge weight rather than distance from the root.

00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:06.960
On dense complete graphs, an array-based O(V squared) Prim loop avoids sorting millions of edges.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:14.673
Every path inside a Minimum Spanning Tree automatically minimizes the maximum edge weight along the route.

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:22.638
If you stop Kruskal's algorithm after V minus K merges, you obtain the minimum-cost spanning forest of K clusters.

00:01:22.638 --> 00:01:28.438
Always verify that V minus one edges were chosen to confirm the graph is connected.
